(a) No county official shall solicit or accept directly or indirectly anything of value from any person, corporation, or group which:
(1) Has, or is seeking to obtain contractual or other business or financial relationships with the county;
(2) In exchange for the thing of value seeks to have a county official exercise a matter of discretion in his favor;
(3) In exchange for the thing of value seeks to have interests which may be affected by the performance or nonperformance of the official duty of the county official.
(b) This section shall not apply to campaign contributions which shall be reported in accordance with state law.
(Res. of 1-7-2003, art. A, § 2)

74:
It is the intent of this section that county officials avoid any action whether or not specifically prohibited by section 2-73 which might result in, or create the appearance of the following:
(1) Using public office for private gain;
(2) Impeding government efficiency or economy;
(3) Affecting adversely the confidence of the public in the integrity of the government; or
(4) Purposefully or intentionally violating laws involving moral turpitude as defined by state law.
(Res. of 1-7-2003, art. A, § 3)

82:
A county official shall not use his superior position to request or require an employee to:
(1) Do clerical work on behalf of his family, business, social, church or fraternal interests;
(2) Purchase goods and services to be used for personal, business or political purposes;
(3) Work for him personally without offering just compensation;
(4) Perform work allegedly for the benefit of the county without prior official authorization.
(Res. of 1-7-2003, art. A, § 11)

205:
The following factors shall be considered in determining whether or not to grant a malt beverage and/or wine license:
(1) The proximity of other licensed malt beverage and/or wine sales outlets to the proposed business location;
(2) The nature of the neighborhood immediately adjacent to the proposed business location; that is, whether the same is predominantly residential, commercial or industrial;
(3) The proximity of churches, hospitals, schools, college campuses, public parks, playgrounds and private residences;
(4) The availability of adequate off street parking facilities for patrons;
(5) The possibility of increased traffic congestion and traffic hazards;
(6) The criminal record, financial responsibility, character and reputation of the applicant, owners or stockholders of the applicant, and proposed outlet manager;
(7) The compliance with all application and advertisement requirements;
(8) The need for additional business facilities selling malt beverages within the county;
(9) The revocation for cause of any previous license for the sale of malt beverages and/or wine issued to the applicant or proposed outlet manager; or for the proposed business location;
(10) The history or reputation of the proposed business location for prostitution or other sex offenses, fighting, shooting, stabbing or violence, gambling, illegal drug dealing in alcoholic beverages or drugs, or other violations of the law.
(Res. of 1-16-1966, § 9; Res. No. 2019-08A, § 1, 4-9-2019)

206:
(a) Denial of license. An application for a malt beverage and/or wine license may be denied if it is determined based upon the factors set forth in section 6-27 that the granting of such license would be detrimental to the public safety, health or welfare of the residents of the county.
(b) Prohibited persons. An application for a malt beverage and/or wine license shall be denied to the following:
(1) An individual who is an illegal alien;
(2) A corporation which is not duly qualified to do business in the state or whose proposed manager is a prohibited person under the terms of this section;
(3) A person who has been convicted of a felony, of a crime opposed to decency and morality or of a crime involving the violation of any county, state or federal laws or regulations pertaining to the manufacture, possession, transportation or sale of malt beverages, wine or intoxicating liquors and served any sentence imposed following such a conviction within a period of five years prior to the date of application;
(4) A person whose license has been revoked for cause or whose license for the sale of malt beverages and/or wine issued by any other licensing authority has been revoked within a period of five years prior to the date of application;
(5) An individual who is under the age of 21 or who is not of sound mind and memory;
(6) An officer or employee of the county, or members of their immediate families; provided that the subsequent election, appointment, or employment shall not result in the revocation or prevent the renewal of a malt beverage and/or wine license previously granted.
(c) Prohibited locations.
(1) No original retail or wholesale malt beverage and/or wine license, including consumption on premises or retail package license, shall be issued under this article for any location which is within 500 feet of a church, public or private school or college campus, or a private residence on the same street unless such residence is itself in a commercial district.
(2) The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this subsection, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Church building means the main structure used by any religious organization for purposes of worship.
Private residence means a house, dwelling, condominium or duplex wherein not less than one nor more than two families reside and shall not include a mobile home park or court, an apartment house having facilities for housing more than two families, nor a boardinghouse or roominghouse where there are five or more boarders or roomers. Any building occupied as a residence located within an area zoned for business shall not be construed as a "private residence."
School building or educational building means and shall apply only to state, county, city, or church school buildings and to such buildings at such other schools in which are taught subjects commonly taught in the common schools and colleges of this state and which are public schools and private schools as defined in O.C.G.A. § 20-2-690(b).
(3) For purposes of this section, distance shall be measured by the most direct route of travel on the ground and shall be measured in the following manner:
a. From the main entrance of the establishment from which malt beverages are sold or offered for sale;
b. In a straight line to the nearest public sidewalk, street, road or highway right-of-way by the nearest route;
c. Along such public sidewalk, street, road or highway right-of-way by the nearest route;
d. To the nearest property line of a church building, private residence, or school grounds;
e. To a point at the front door of the main structure of the church building, school, or residential building, other than the dwelling of the licensee.
(4) No location which is licensed to sell alcoholic beverages on the effective date of the ordinance from which this section is derived shall be denied continued operation under an existing license, or denied any renewal of such license, nor shall any new owner of the location be denied a new license based upon the measurements set forth in this section.
(5) As to any location licensed in the future, if the distance requirements in this section are met at the time of issuance of any license, the subsequent opening and operation of a church or school, or construction of a private residence within the distance prohibited in this section shall not prevent the continuance of an existing license or the renewal thereof or the issuance of a new license to any subsequent owner of such property. Provided, however, that the distance requirements shall not apply at any location for which a new license is applied if the sale of alcoholic beverages was lawful at such location at any time during the 12 months immediately preceding such application.
(6) Where a license is issued to a grocery or convenience store, where the sale of malt beverages and/or wine is incidental to the sale of groceries and other like items, such store shall be exempt from the provisions relating to private residences.
(Res. of 1-16-1966, § 10; Res. of 12-1-1992; Res. of 6-14-2004(1), § 10; Res. of 6-14-2004(2), § 3; Ord. of 4-7-2009(1); Ord. No. 2018-09, § 1, of 6-5-2018; Ord. No. 2017-04, § 2, 10-9-2017; Res. No. 2019-08A, § 1, 4-9-2019)
