218:
(a) No holder of a retail package store, pouring outlet, or alcoholic beverage caterer license, nor any agent or employee of said license holder, shall do any of the following upon the outlet premises:
(1) Knowingly sell malt beverages and/or wine to any underage person as provided in (O.C.G.A. § 3-3-23);
(2) Knowingly sell malt beverages and/or wine to any person who is in a state of noticeable intoxication;
(3) Sell malt beverages and/or wine or permit the consumption of malt beverages and/or wine on any day or at any time when such sale or consumption is prohibited;
(4) Allow any disorderly conduct, breach of the peace, or any lewd, immoral or illegal conduct, entertainment or practices on the licensed premises;
(5) Allow "curb service" or the consumption of malt beverages and/or wine on any parking lot adjacent to the premises.
(b) Malt beverages and/or wine shall be received at and delivered to the premises of a retail package store or pouring outlet licensee only by conveyance owned and operated by a wholesale distributor licensed in the county, unless said wholesale distributor is licensed in another county of this state and only delivers and receives payment in the county for malt beverages and/or wine previously ordered through a representative of said wholesale distributor.
(c) Each holder of a license issued pursuant to this article shall display the license prominently at all times on the premises for which the license is issued.
(d) Every retail package store and pouring outlet shall post in a conspicuous place on the premises a sign in letters at least two inches high, reading, "Sale of malt beverages and/or wine to minors strictly prohibited."
(e) No screen, partition or other object which prevents a clear view into the interior of a retail package store from the street shall be permitted.
(f) Regulations of the state department of revenue relating to the sale and distribution of malt beverages and/or wine are hereby incorporated into and made a part of this article as if fully set out herein.
(Res. of 1-16-1966, § 26; Res. of 8-6-2013; Res. No. 2019-08A, § 1, 4-9-2019)

240:
(a) In addition to any other remedies provided in this chapter, the animal control officer may seize and impound at the animal shelter any of the following animals:
(1) Any dog or cat without a valid rabies tag and identifying tag;
(2) Any animal constituting a public nuisance or considered a danger to the public as determined by the animal control officer;
(3) Any animal in violation of any quarantine or confinement order issued by the county board of health or the public health director;
(4) Any unattended animal that is ill, injured or otherwise in need of care;
(5) Any animal that is believed to have been abused or neglected as determined by the animal control officer;
(6) Any animal that is reasonably suspected of having rabies;
(7) Any animal that a court of competent jurisdiction has ordered impounded or destroyed;
(8) Any animal that is considered unattended or abandoned, as in situations where the owner is deceased or has been arrested or evicted from his regular place of residence and there is no other person who will take custody of the animal.
(9) Any owner may request live pickup of animal if it is determined that animal cannot be adequately cared for or if other attempts for alternative placement have failed. The animal control department has complete authority to approve or disapprove live animal pick up and types of animals that will be considered for pickup. If approved, owner must pay appropriate fee as referenced in Subpart A, Chapter 1, Section 1-17 - Fee Schedule. Any owner who requests live pickup of an animal must immediately and in writing relinquish all ownership rights of animal. The owner shall have no right to reclaim an animal that has been relinquished to animal control.
(b) The animal control officer or appropriate law enforcement officer may also, or in lieu of impoundment, issue to the owner a notice of violation. Such notice will cite the owner to appear on a date certain before the magistrate court of the county as provided in this chapter.
(c) Any person finding an animal at large upon his property may remove the same to the animal shelter or hold the animal in his own possession, and as soon as possible, notify animal control. The property owner shall provide a description of the animal and the name of the owner, if known. Animal control shall dispatch an animal control officer to take possession of the animal as soon as possible.
(d) Any animal control officer or other designated person who seizes and impounds an animal that cannot be housed at the animal shelter shall be authorized to arrange transportation of the animal to a farm or other appropriate facility which agrees to accept such animal. The disposition of the animal shall be handled in the same manner as though the animal were confined at the animal shelter except that, in addition to the fees charged for redemption as referenced in Subpart A, Chapter 1, Section 1-17 - Fee Schedule, the owner shall also pay the actual transportation and feeding costs incurred while said animal was impounded.
(e) Any animal control officer or other person who discovers or is notified of livestock (as that term is defined in O.C.G.A. § 4-3-2) running at large, shall notify the appropriate law enforcement agency. The seizure, impoundment and disposition of such livestock shall be in accordance with the provisions of O.C.G.A. title 4, ch. 3 (O.C.G.A. § 4-3-1 et seq.).
(Ord. of 12-2-2003, § XIV; Ord. of 9-1-2015; Ord. No. 2019-04, § 1, 2-5-2019)
